# Target C-II — loaded-unit endpoints force unloaded attachment

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Status:** proof-only structural theorem; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** canonical full-core near-disk C-II branch; low-`t` constants use the proof-only minimax atomic gap `0.0021` / §171  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The boundary-atom inequality

\[
N_2+2e_{BB}\ge3q-b
\]

still contains `e_BB`, the number of exact unit contacts between consecutive loaded
force-hull vertices.  This note eliminates that currency into actual loaded-to-unloaded
attachments and the already extracted double-unit-corner geometry.

Let `C_11` be the number of loaded hull vertices whose two adjacent hull sides are
both unit contacts.  Let `J_BI` be the number of distinct loaded-to-unloaded contact
edges obtained from loaded hull vertices incident to exactly one loaded-unit hull
side.  Let

\[
s_B=\sum_{b\in B}(3-\deg_Cb)
\]

and let `E_in` be the strongly inward loaded-to-unloaded contacts supplied by the
double-unit-corner theorem.

The exact new conclusions are

\[
\boxed{J_{BI}\ge2(e_{BB}-C_{11}),}
\tag{UA.1}
\]

\[
\boxed{e_{BB}\le C_{11}+{J_{BI}\over2},}
\tag{UA.2}
\]

and therefore, using `s_B+|E_in|>=C_11-6`,

\[
\boxed{
N_2+J_{BI}+2s_B+2|E_{in}|
\ge3q-b-12.
}
\tag{UA.3}
\]

Thus the large loaded-unit boundary branch cannot remain a boundary-only currency.
It must create loaded-to-unloaded contact attachment, loaded degree slack, or strongly
inward attachment at linear scale.

## 1. A loaded endpoint of a unit hull side cannot have contact degree one

Let `AB` be a unit side of the loaded force hull `F`, and orient a unit normal `n`
strictly into `F` from the supporting line through `AB`.

Take the canonical relative-interior full-support KKT stress.  Since `A` is loaded,
it is incident to at least one actual diameter edge `AX` with positive multiplier.
Every actual diameter endpoint belongs to the loaded vertex set `B=Vert(F)`.
Because `AB` is a side of the convex polygon `F`, every loaded vertex other than
`A,B` lies strictly in the inward half-plane.  A diameter partner `X` cannot equal
`B`, since `|A-B|=1<D` for the asymptotic branch.  Hence

\[
(X-A)\cdot n>0,
\qquad
(A-X)\cdot n<0.
\tag{UA.4}
\]

All actual diameter multipliers are positive in the full-support normalization, so the
reaction vector

\[
g_A:=\sum_{AX\in H}\beta_{AX}(A-X)
\]

satisfies

\[
\boxed{g_A\cdot n<0.}
\tag{UA.5}
\]

KKT equilibrium at `A` is

\[
g_A=\sum_{AU\in C}\alpha_{AU}(A-U),
\qquad \alpha_{AU}>0.
\tag{UA.6}
\]

If `AB` were the only contact incident to `A`, the right side of (UA.6) would be
`alpha_AB(A-B)`, tangent to the support line, and therefore would have zero dot product
with `n`.  This contradicts (UA.5).

Hence:

\[
\boxed{
\text{every endpoint of an exact loaded-unit hull side has contact degree at least two.}
}
\tag{UA.7}
\]

This argument is finite.  It uses neither a multiplier lower bound nor an asymptotic
near-circle direction estimate.

## 2. A single-unit endpoint must attach to an unloaded vertex

Canonical loaded-chord elimination says every loaded-loaded unit contact is a side of
the loaded force hull.  Therefore the loaded-unit contact graph is exactly a subgraph
of the cyclic hull-side graph.

Call a loaded hull vertex **single-unit** when exactly one of its two hull sides is a
unit contact.  Let `V_1` be the number of such vertices.

Fix a single-unit vertex `A`, and let `AB` be its unique unit loaded-hull side.  By
(UA.7), `A` has another contact.  If an additional contact joined `A` to another
loaded vertex, loaded-chord elimination would make that contact the other hull side,
contradicting the definition of single-unit.  Hence every additional contact at `A`
is loaded-to-unloaded.

Choose one such contact from every single-unit vertex.  The chosen edges are distinct:
a loaded-to-unloaded edge has only one loaded endpoint, and the selected loaded
endpoints are distinct.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{J_{BI}\ge V_1.}
\tag{UA.8}
\]

## 3. Exact cycle handshake

In the loaded-unit hull-side graph every loaded vertex has degree zero, one, or two.
The degree-two vertices are exactly the `C_11` double-unit corners; the degree-one
vertices are exactly the `V_1` single-unit vertices.  Handshake gives

\[
2e_{BB}=V_1+2C_{11}.
\tag{UA.9}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{V_1=2(e_{BB}-C_{11}).}
\tag{UA.10}
\]

Combining (UA.8) and (UA.10) proves (UA.1), and rearranging gives (UA.2).

The count has no matching loss: every single-unit endpoint itself supplies an
unloaded attachment.

## 4. Eliminate `e_BB` from the low-`t` boundary atom ledger

The exact boundary-atom theorem gives

\[
\boxed{N_2+2e_{BB}\ge3q-b.}
\tag{UA.11}
\]

The double-unit-corner theorem gives

\[
\boxed{s_B+|E_{in}|\ge C_{11}-6.}
\tag{UA.12}
\]

Equation (UA.2) and (UA.12) imply

\[
\begin{aligned}
2e_{BB}
&\le2C_{11}+J_{BI}\\
&\le2s_B+2|E_{in}|+12+J_{BI}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{UA.13}
\]

Insert (UA.13) into the left side of (UA.11).  Because the replacement is an upper
bound for the same positive currency,

\[
N_2+J_{BI}+2s_B+2|E_{in}|+12
\ge N_2+2e_{BB}
\ge3q-b.
\]

This proves (UA.3).

Every term on the left of (UA.3) now has direct finite source meaning:

- `N_2`: genuine two-edge outer teeth;
- `J_BI`: distinct unit contacts from single-unit loaded boundary vertices to unloaded
  core vertices;
- `s_B`: loaded contact-degree deficit;
- `E_in`: distinct strongly inward loaded-to-unloaded unit contacts from double-unit
  corners.

## 5. Low-`t` quantitative consequence

On a convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
t={q-k\over D},\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},\qquad
\delta={b-P_F\over D}.
\]

Then

\[
{3q-b\over D}
=3t+3\kappa-\pi-\delta+o(1).
\tag{UA.14}
\]

The current proof-only minimax/§171 squeeze is

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R(t),
\qquad
R(t)={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_0,
\qquad
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0021.
\tag{UA.15}
\]

At fixed `t`, the minimum of `3 kappa-delta` occurs at
`kappa=0`, `delta=R(t)/2`.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {3q-b\over D}
\ge 3t-\pi-{R(t)\over2}
={3t\over4}+{C_0\over2}.
}
\tag{UA.16}
\]

Divide (UA.3) by `D`.  The finite constant `12` disappears, and if

\[
M_{phys}:=\max\{N_2,J_{BI},s_B,|E_{in}|\},
\]

then the weighted left side of (UA.3) is at most `6M_phys`.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {M_{phys}\over D}
\ge {t\over8}+{C_0\over12}.
}
\tag{UA.17}
\]

At the current proof-only floor

\[
t_{21}={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right)+0.0017279,
\]

this gives approximately

\[
\boxed{
M_{phys}\ge0.38654898\,D-o(D).
}
\tag{UA.18}
\]

This is more than twice the previous three-currency floor from the double-unit-corner
note.  The improvement comes from using every endpoint of the loaded-unit side graph,
not only vertices where two unit sides meet.

## 6. Relation to the near-diameter switch theorem

The near-diameter switch theorem gives a different refinement of the same `e_BB`
currency: a large matching of loaded-unit contacts forces either shell wedges in the
actual diameter graph or linearly many inactive `D-O(D^{-1/4})` source pairs.

The present theorem is orthogonal and finite at contact scale.  Even in the shell-wedge
regression, every single-unit endpoint still needs an unloaded contact unless it
continues into a double-unit boundary corner.  Therefore the regular-shell local model
can persist only by paying either boundary continuation or core attachment.

A closing theorem can now attack either side:

1. use the `J_BI/E_in` population to force contact/train area or response cost; or
2. use the near-active diameter switches to force an active-set exchange or global
   packing loss.

## 7. Scope audit

- The strict normal component in (UA.5) uses that `B=Vert(F)` and that `AB` is a hull
  side; no smoothness or near-circle approximation is used.
- Full-support KKT positivity is used only to make at least one strict diameter normal
  contribution survive.
- Loaded-chord elimination is used exactly once: an extra loaded contact at a
  single-unit endpoint would have to be the other hull side.
- The cycle handshake (UA.9) is exact because all loaded-loaded contacts are hull
  sides in the full-core near-disk branch.
- `J_BI` is a set of distinct physical unit contacts, not an occurrence count.
- Contact-isolated source points are retained and do not enter the positive-core
  incidence count.
- The low-`t` decimal inherits the proof-only minimax-atomic-gap lifecycle.
- No exact unit equilateral triangle or strict same-cardinality sub-`D` competitor has
  been produced.

**Disposition:** proof-only structural theorem.  C-II and Level I remain open.
